          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "For heart failure, is there a code that has to come before or after it, and why?",
          "answer": "**Yes, for hypertensive heart disease.** When the record says the heart disease is hypertensive or due to hypertension, the combination code I11 (hypertensive heart disease) is reported first. A heart failure code from I50 is then added to show the type of heart failure. The heart failure code is added to the hypertension code, not used in its place. (Sources: *Coding heart failure: I50* and *Coding hypertension: I10*.)\n\nThe reason is the \"code first\" and \"use additional code\" convention. An underlying condition is reported before its manifestation, so the cause comes first and the effect is added after it. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n\nThe hypertension rules also work differently for the heart and the kidneys. The classification presumes a link between hypertension and chronic kidney disease, but it does not presume one for heart disease. Unless the record states that the heart condition is hypertensive or due to hypertension, the two are coded separately and I11 is not used. (Source: *Coding hypertension: I10*.)\n\nThe documents I searched did not list every code-first or use-additional note that applies to I50. For other possible underlying causes, such as valve disease or cardiomyopathy, I could not confirm the sequencing rules from these passages. A medical coder or the official ICD-10-CM tabular list would give the complete rules.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
